T1  - Shelter proximity and affect among homeless smokers making a quit attempt.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.5993/AJHB.38.2.1
A1  - Reitzel, Lorraine R
A1  - Kendzor, Darla E
A1  - Nguyen, Nga
A1  - Regan, Seann D
A1  - Okuyemi, Kolawole S
A1  - Castro, Yessenia
A1  - Wetter, David W
A1  - Businelle, Michael S
Y1  - 2014//
N2  - OBJECTIVES: To explore the associations between shelter proximity and real-time affect during a specific smoking quit attempt among 22 homeless adults., METHODS: Affect was measured via 485 smartphone-based Ecological Momentary Assessments randomly administered during the weeks immediately before and after the quit day, and proximity to the shelter was measured via GPS. Adjusted linear mixed model regressions examined associations between shelter proximity and affect., RESULTS: Closer proximity to the shelter was associated with greater negative affect only during the post-quit attempt week (p = .008). All participants relapsed to smoking by one week post-quit attempt., CONCLUSIONS: Among homeless smokers trying to quit, the shelter may be associated with unexpected negative affect/stress. Potential intervention applications are suggested.
