T1  - Associations between proximity and density of local alcohol outlets and alcohol use among Scottish adolescents.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.healthplace.2012.10.004
A1  - Young, Robert
A1  - Macdonald, Laura
A1  - Ellaway, Anne
Y1  - 2013//
Y2  - 20121115//
N2  - Associations between different alcohol outcomes and outlet density measures vary between studies and may not be generalisable to adolescents. In a cross-sectional study of 979 15-year old Glaswegians, we investigated the association between alcohol outlet availability (outlet density and proximity), outlet type (on-premise vs. off-premise) and frequent (weekly) alcohol consumption. We adjusted for social background (gender, social class, family structure). Proximity and density of on-premise outlets were not associated with weekly drinking. However, adolescents living close (within 200 m) to an off-sales outlet were more likely to drink frequently (OR 1.97, p=0.004), as were adolescents living in areas with many nearby off-premises outlets (OR 1.60, p=0.016). Our findings suggest that certain alcohol behaviours (e.g. binge drinking) may be linked to the characteristics of alcohol outlets in the vicinity. Copyright © 2012 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
