A1  - Sturk, Heidi
A1  - Kavanagh, David
A1  - Gallois, Cindy
A1  - King, David
A1  - Turpin, Merrill
A1  - King, Robert
A1  - Bartlett, Helen
Y1  - 2007//
N2  - OBJECTIVE: The aim of this paper was to examine self-efficacy and perceived appropriateness among rural general practitioners (GPs) in regards to screening and intervention for physical, lifestyle and mental health issues., METHOD: Fifty GPs from 25 practices in eight rural Queensland towns completed a written survey designed for the study., RESULTS: General practitioners rated opportunistic screening or assessment for smoking and for detection of relapse of mental disorders as the most appropriate, with even cardiovascular and diabetes risk falling behind these. Self-efficacy was highest for medical disorders for smoking assessment. It was significantly lower for alcohol, mental health issues, and addressing risks of physical disorder in people with mental disorders., CONCLUSIONS: High appropriateness ratings suggest that current strategies to boost self-efficacy of GPs in addressing mental health issues are timely.
