T1  - Convergent validity of measures of cognitive distortions, impulsivity, and time perspective with pathological gambling.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1037/0893-164X.20.1.75
A1  - Mackillop, James
A1  - Anderson, Emily J
A1  - Castelda, Bryan A
A1  - Mattson, Richard E
A1  - Donovick, Peter J
Y1  - 2006//
N2  - The present study investigated the convergent validity of the Gamblers' Beliefs Questionnaire (GBQ), Gambling Passion Scale (GPS), Eysenck Impulsivity Questionnaire (EIQ), and Stanford Time Perception Inventory (STPI) in reference to pathological gambling. The authors recruited 105 undergraduates representing categories of pathological gamblers, potential pathological gamblers, and nonpathological gamblers and administered the measures under neutral conditions. Both subscales of the GBQ and GPS and the Impulsivity subscale of the EIQ exhibited strong convergent validity, whereas the STPI showed weaker correspondence with symptoms of pathological gambling. Applications and limitations of these findings are discussed.
