T1  - Comparison of the smoking behaviour and attitudes of smokers who believe they have smoking-related problems with those who do not.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1093/fampra/cmg504
A1  - Coleman, Tim
A1  - Barrett, Stephen
A1  - Wynn, Alison
A1  - Wilson, Andrew
Y1  - 2003//
N2  - BACKGROUND: Motivation to stop smoking is associated with smokers' possessing substantial smoking-related morbidity or believing that they have symptoms caused by smoking, but it is not clear if this holds for smokers attending general practice consultations., OBJECTIVE: Our aim was to compare the attitudes and behaviour of smokers attending their GP with symptoms that they believe are smoking related with those who do not., METHOD: A cross-sectional, pre-consultation survey of patients attending GPs in Leicester, UK was carried out., RESULTS: A total of 83.8% (2955/3525) of people attending GPs completed the questionnaire and 34.7% were smokers. Multiple logistic regression showed that where smokers perceived that their problems were smoking related they were more likely to have tried stopping in the past [odds ratio (OR) 1.78, 95% confidence interval (CI) 1.26-2.67], to want to stop smoking (OR 1.83, CI 1.15-2.9) or to intend to stop in the near future (OR 1.58, CI 1.03-2.43)., CONCLUSION: Smokers who attend GPs' routine consultations and believe that they have smoking-related problems are more motivated to stop than others. This suggests that it is important for GPs to ascertain patients' views about the aetiology of their symptoms before discussing smoking with them.
